\pard\sb0\sa240\qc\b\f0\fs32\cf1 MARKER SPACE CHECK\par
\pard\sb0\sa80\keepn\b\f0\fs22\cf2 PURPOSE\par
\pard\sb0\sa120\b0\f0\fs20\cf0 Scans selected books for character-style markers (read from usfm.sty and the project's custom.sty), plus {\f1\\f} (footnote) and {\f1\\x} (cross-reference), and flags spacing problems around each opening/closing marker pair. Each occurrence is reported both as a results-list entry (with reference and text snippet, like other checks) and as a line in a detailed HTML report ({\f1 MarkerSpaceReport.html}), written to the project folder and opened automatically when the check finishes.\par
\pard\sb0\sa120\f0\fs20 Marker codes starting with {\f1\\t} are ignored by default (table/structure markers such as {\f1\\tc1} or {\f1\\tr}), except {\f1\\tl} (a genuine character-style pair) and {\f1\\ts-s}/{\f1\\ts-e} (USFM 3 section milestones), which are never silently excluded even though they aren't independently spacing-checked by this tool. Cross-references already use the "before the word" convention by default (see CrossReferencesPrecedeText below), unlike footnotes (see FootnotePrecedesText).\par
\pard\sb240\sa80\keepn\b\f0\fs22\cf2 CHECKS PERFORMED\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\b0\f0\fs20 1.\tab{\b Missing space before opening marker}\emdash e.g. {\f1 word\\nd} instead of {\f1 word \\nd}. Exempt when the marker's own content is itself just trailing punctuation (e.g. {\f1 metal\\ft .\\f*}) -- the marker is invisible once rendered, so gluing it to the preceding word is what's needed for that punctuation to render correctly tight against it. Does NOT apply to closing markers -- see check 5.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\f0\fs20 2.\tab{\b Unwanted space before opening marker}\emdash for markers listed in NoSpaceBeforeMarkers (default {\f1\\f}, {\f1\\x}), which conventionally attach directly to the preceding word with no space.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\f0\fs20 3.\tab{\b Unwanted space before closing marker}\emdash e.g. {\f1\\nd word \\nd*} instead of {\f1\\nd word\\nd*}.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\f0\fs20 4.\tab{\b Missing space after closing marker}\emdash the closing marker is glued directly to the following word with no space.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\f0\fs20 5.\tab{\b Unwanted space before punctuation after closing marker}\emdash e.g. {\f1\\nd* ,} instead of {\f1\\nd*,}. This rule is the same regardless of any other option.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\f0\fs20 6.\tab{\b Unclosed marker}\emdash an opening marker with no matching closing marker found before the end of the chapter.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\f0\fs20 7.\tab{\b Unexpected closing marker}\emdash a closing marker found for a marker listed in NoCloseMarkers (markers that don't normally take one at all, e.g. {\f1\\v}, {\f1\\xk}, {\f1\\fp}).\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\f0\fs20 8.\tab{\b Missing space before a NoCloseMarkers marker}\emdash e.g. {\f1\\fr 1:13\\fq} instead of {\f1\\fr 1:13 \\fq}. Same idea as check 1, applied to markers that never take a closing marker, including the same trailing-punctuation exemption.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\f0\fs20 9.\tab{\b Space before | inside \\w...\\w*}\emdash e.g. {\f1\\w Word |Word in glossary\\w*} instead of {\f1\\w Word|Word in glossary\\w*}. {\f1\\w} optionally carries a |-delimited glossary link after the displayed word; a space right before the | becomes part of the rendered text and produces a double space. A {\f1\\w...\\w*} with no | at all is unaffected.\par
\pard\sb0\sa120\f0\fs20{\i Note:} punctuation directly after a closing marker is always normal (never flagged), and so is a closing marker immediately followed by another closing marker (nested character styles ending together, e.g. {\f1\\+nd*\\add*}) -- any real problem there belongs to that outer marker's own check.\par
\pard\sb0\sa120\f0\fs20{\i Note:} every check above treats {\f1 ~} (Paratext's non-breaking-space character) exactly like a normal space, in both directions -- a {\f1 ~} satisfies a check expecting a space (e.g. no false "missing space" for {\f1 word~\\nd}), and a {\f1 ~} trips a check flagging an unwanted space, the same as a real space would.\par
\pard\sb240\sa80\keepn\b\f0\fs22\cf2 HOW TO USE\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\b0\f0\fs20 1.\tab Install the Phils Custom (cms) Checks plugin with Install_Phils_tools.exe. Everything comes with the plugin: nothing has to be copied into the cms folder. References in the HTML report can be clicked to show that place in Paratext. The first click makes the browser ask whether to open ParatextGoTo, and Paratext follows only when the text window's scroll group follows other programs.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\f0\fs20 2.\tab In Paratext, choose Tools > Phils Custom (cms) Checks..., select this check and click "Run...". In Paratext's check window, choose the books and set the options (Options...) before you click OK.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sb0\sa80\f0\fs20 3.\tab Review the results list for each flagged occurrence, then open the full HTML report (written to the project folder and launched automatically) for summary counts and grouped examples.\par
\pard\sb240\sa80\keepn\b\f0\fs22\cf2 OPTIONS\par
\pard\li360\sb160\sa0\keepn\b\f1\fs20\cf3 ExcludeMarkers\par
\pard\li720\sb60\sa0\b0\f0\fs20{\b Default:}  {\i (empty)}\par
\pard\li720\sb40\sa80\f0\fs20 Marker codes, separated by spaces, to skip entirely (e.g.  w rq ), for markers that follow a different spacing convention in this project.\par
\pard\li360\sb160\sa0\keepn\b\f1\fs20\cf3 NoCloseMarkers\par
\pard\li720\sb60\sa0\b0\f0\fs20{\b Default:}  {\i v fk fp fq fqa ft fr xk xo xq}\par
\pard\li720\sb40\sa80\f0\fs20 Marker codes, separated by spaces, that don't normally take a closing marker in any context. These are skipped by checks 2-5 above (there's no closing marker to check); they're still checked for a missing space before them (check 8), and if a closing marker is nonetheless found for one, it's reported separately (check 7).\par
\pard\li360\sb160\sa0\keepn\b\f1\fs20\cf3 NoCloseInNoteMarkers\par
\pard\li720\sb60\sa0\b0\f0\fs20{\b Default:}  {\i xt}\par
\pard\li720\sb40\sa80\f0\fs20 Marker codes, separated by spaces, that only skip being flagged as unclosed when nested inside a footnote ({\f1\\f...\\f*}) or cross-reference ({\f1\\x...\\x*}) span with no closing marker of their own. If one of these DOES carry its own closing marker there, it's still fully checked (checks 1, 3-5) like any other marker. Used on their own outside a footnote/cross-reference, they're always checked normally, including requiring their own closing marker.\par
\pard\li360\sb160\sa0\keepn\b\f1\fs20\cf3 ExtraSpacingMarkers\par
\pard\li720\sb60\sa0\b0\f0\fs20{\b Default:}  {\i f x}\par
\pard\li720\sb40\sa80\f0\fs20 Marker codes, separated by spaces, to check for spacing even though they aren't StyleType Character in usfm.sty. {\f1\\f} and {\f1\\x} are Note-type markers by default but should still follow normal open/close spacing conventions.\par
\pard\li360\sb160\sa0\keepn\b\f1\fs20\cf3 NoSpaceBeforeMarkers\par
\pard\li720\sb60\sa0\b0\f0\fs20{\b Default:}  {\i f x}\par
\pard\li720\sb40\sa0\f0\fs20 Marker codes, separated by spaces, that conventionally attach directly to the preceding word or punctuation with no space (footnote and cross-reference callers). For these, a space before the marker is the anomaly instead of the usual missing-space check.\par
\pard\li720\sb0\sa80\f0\fs20{\f1\\f} is automatically removed from this list when FootnotePrecedesText (below) is set to Yes; {\f1\\x} is automatically removed from this list when CrossReferencesPrecedeText (below) is set to Yes (the default).\par
\pard\li360\sb160\sa0\keepn\b\f1\fs20\cf3 FootnotePrecedesText\par
\pard\li720\sb60\sa0\b0\f0\fs20{\b Default:}  {\i No}\par
\pard\li720\sb40\sa80\f0\fs20 Set to Yes if this project's convention places the footnote marker BEFORE the word it annotates, rather than after. Reverses the {\f1\\f} spacing checks: a space is expected before {\f1\\f}, and no space is expected after {\f1\\f*} (a stray space directly before punctuation is still always flagged either way -- see check 5). Does not affect {\f1\\x} -- see CrossReferencesPrecedeText below for that marker.\par
\pard\li360\sb160\sa0\keepn\b\f1\fs20\cf3 CrossReferencesPrecedeText ({\i localized:} Cross-references occur before word)\par
\pard\li720\sb60\sa0\b0\f0\fs20{\b Default:}  {\i Yes}\par
\pard\li720\sb40\sa80\f0\fs20 Set to Yes (the default) if this project's convention places the cross-reference marker BEFORE the word it annotates, rather than after. Reverses the {\f1\\x} spacing checks: a space is expected before {\f1\\x}, and no space is expected after {\f1\\x*} (a stray space directly before punctuation is still always flagged either way -- see check 5). Set to No if this project instead glues {\f1\\x} directly to the preceding word/punctuation with no space, the traditional convention {\f1\\f} still uses by default. Does not affect {\f1\\f} -- see FootnotePrecedesText above for that marker.\par
\pard\li360\sb160\sa0\keepn\b\f1\fs20\cf3 MaxExamples\par
\pard\li720\sb60\sa0\b0\f0\fs20{\b Default:}  {\i 25}\par
\pard\li720\sb40\sa80\f0\fs20 Caps how many example references are listed under each section of the HTML report. Total counts shown in the report are unaffected.\par
